/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "⟨10,2⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186721560}] [{:answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "⟨7,0⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186721562}] [{:answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-42" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186721563}] [{:answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "⟨11,2⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186721564}] [{:answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "⟨17,-5⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186721566}] [{:answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186721567}] [{:answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186721568}] [{:answer/feedback "The interval from $0$ to $\\pi$ covers only half of the ellipse." :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-3\\int_0^{\\pi}(2\\sin t+3\\cos t+1)\\sin t\\,\\mathrm{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186721584}] [{:answer/feedback "This uses the derivative of $x(t)$ instead of the derivative of $y(t)$." :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3\\int_0^1(5t-2)\\,\\mathrm{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186721587}] [{:answer/feedback "The substitution, limits, and signed $y$ derivative are correct." :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\int_0^1(5t-2)\\,\\mathrm{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186721588}] [{:answer/feedback "This follows one clockwise traversal and includes the signed $y$ derivative." :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-3\\int_0^{2\\pi}(2\\sin t+3\\cos t+1)\\sin t\\,\\mathrm{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186721589}] [{:answer/feedback "The parametrization follows the specified arc and uses $y\\prime(t)=\\cos t$." :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\int_{\\pi/2}^{\\pi}(4\\cos^2 t+\\sin t)\\cos t\\,\\mathrm{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186721590}] [{:answer/feedback "The derivative of $3\\cos t$ is $-3\\sin t$, so this expression has the wrong sign." :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3\\int_0^{2\\pi}(2\\sin t+3\\cos t+1)\\sin t\\,\\mathrm{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186721591}] [{:answer/feedback "This uses $x\\prime(t)=-2\\sin t$, which belongs to $\\mathrm{d}x$." :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2\\int_{\\pi/2}^{\\pi}(4\\cos^2 t+\\sin t)\\sin t\\,\\mathrm{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186721592}] [{:answer/feedback "The factor $y\\prime(t)=2$ is missing." :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\int_0^1(5t-2)\\,\\mathrm{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186721593}] [{:answer/feedback "Substituting into $x+y$ gives $5t-2$, not $t-2$." :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\int_0^1(t-2)\\,\\mathrm{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186721594}] [{:answer/feedback "The factor $y\\prime(t)=-3\\sin t$ is missing." :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\int_0^{2\\pi}(2\\sin t+3\\cos t+1)\\,\\mathrm{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186721595}] [{:answer/feedback "The signed derivative is $\\cos t$, which is negative on this interval. Replacing it by its absolute value reverses the sign." :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\int_{\\pi/2}^{\\pi}(4\\cos^2 t+\\sin t)\\cos t\\,\\mathrm{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186721596}] [{:answer/feedback "A closed path does not imply that a line integral with a general nonconstant integrand is zero. The endpoint rule for an integral of unity does not apply here." :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186721599}] [{:answer/feedback "These limits follow the lower-right quarter of the ellipse instead of the specified upper-left quarter." :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\int_{-\\pi/2}^{0}(4\\cos^2 t+\\sin t)\\cos t\\,\\mathrm{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186721600}] [{:answer/feedback "The $y$ derivative is missing." :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\int_{\\pi/2}^{\\